\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A unit fraction is \(1/x\) with \(x\in\mathbb N\).
The three denominators below need not be distinct; no order or
coprimality condition is imposed on them.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every integer \(n\ge2\), do there exist positive integers
\(x,y,z\) such that
\[
 \frac4n=\frac1x+\frac1y+\frac1z?
\] \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.1100}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can four divided by every integer at least two be written as a sum of three positive unit fractions, with repeated denominators allowed?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 1158 (NT-024). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Eugen J. Ionascu and Andrew Wilson, \emph{On the Erd\H{o}s--Straus conjecture}, arXiv:1001.1100 (2010), abstract and Section 1. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.1100}.
\end{description}
